.TH DWM 1 dwm\-VERSION
.SH NAME
dwm \- dynamic window manager
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B dwm
.RB [ \-v ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
dwm is a dynamic window manager for X. It manages windows in tiling layouts, including nrowgrid, grid, bottom stack, gapless grid, and horizontal grid. Each layout can be applied dynamically, optimising the
environment for the application in use and the task performed.
.P
There are five layouts: four grids and bottom stack.
The default layout is nrowgrid (###). Other layouts are grid (HHH), bottom
stack (TTT), gapless grid (:::), and horizontal grid (---). Bottom stack places
the master area above the stacking area. Dialog and fixed-size windows are tiled too.
.P
Windows are grouped by tags. Each window can be tagged with one or multiple
tags. Selecting certain tags displays all windows with these tags.
.P
Each screen contains a small status bar which displays all available tags, the
layout, the title of the focused window, and the text read from the root window
name property, if the screen is focused. The selected tags are indicated with a different
color. The tags of the focused window are indicated with a filled square in the
top left corner.  The tags which are applied to one or more windows are
indicated with an empty square in the top left corner.
.P
dwm draws a small border around windows to indicate the focus state.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-v
prints version information to stderr, then exits.
.SH USAGE
.SS Status bar
.TP
.B X root window name
is read and displayed in the status text area. It can be set with the
.BR xsetroot (1)
command.
.TP
.B Button1
click on a tag label to display all windows with that tag, click on the layout
label cycles through the tiling layouts.
.TP
.B Button3
click on a tag label adds/removes all windows with that tag to/from the view.
Click on the layout label to select the previous layout.
.TP
.B Button4 / Button5
Scroll up/down on the layout label to select the previous/next layout.
.TP
.B Super\-Button1
click on a tag label applies that tag to the focused window.
.TP
.B Super\-Button3
click on a tag label adds/removes that tag to/from the focused window.
.SS Keyboard commands
Super refers to the Windows key (Mod4).
.TP
.B Super\-Return
Open ST.
.TP
.B Super\-p
Open Dmenu.
.TP
.B Super\-, / Super\-.
Focus the previous/next monitor.
.TP
.B Super\-Shift\-b
Toggle the bar.
.TP
.B Super\-Shift\-t / y / m
Select nrowgrid (###), grid (HHH), or bottom stack (TTT), respectively.
.TP
.B Super\-[ / Super\-]
Select the previous/next layout. Cycling order is ###, HHH, TTT, :::, ---, then wraps around.
.TP
.B Super\-Left / Right
Move focus; cross tags at window-list boundaries and monitors at tag boundaries.
.TP
.B Super\-Alt\-Left / Right
Reorder the focused window; move it across tags and monitors at boundaries.
.TP
.B Super\-Shift\-Return
Move the focused window to the first position in the tiled window list; if already first, promote the next window.
.TP
.B Super\-x
Close the focused window.
.TP
.B Super\-Alt\-u
Increase gaps.
.TP
.B Super\-Alt\-Shift\-u
Decrease gaps.
.TP
.B Super\-Alt\-0
Toggle gaps.
.TP
.B Super\-[1..5]
View the selected tag.
.TP
.B Super\-Shift\-[1..5]
Move the focused window to the selected tag.
.TP
.B Super\-Control\-[1..5]
Add/remove the selected tag from the view.
.TP
.B Super\-Control\-Shift\-[1..5]
Add/remove the selected tag from the focused window.
.TP
.B Super\-Alt\-q
Quit dwm.
.SH CUSTOMIZATION
dwm is customized by creating a custom config.h and (re)compiling the source
code. This keeps it fast, secure and simple.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR dmenu (1),
.BR st (1)
.SH ISSUES
Java applications which use the XToolkit/XAWT backend may draw grey windows
only. The XToolkit/XAWT backend breaks ICCCM-compliance in recent JDK 1.5 and early
JDK 1.6 versions, because it assumes a reparenting window manager. Possible workarounds
are using JDK 1.4 (which doesn't contain the XToolkit/XAWT backend) or setting the
environment variable
.BR AWT_TOOLKIT=MToolkit
(to use the older Motif backend instead) or running
.B xprop -root -f _NET_WM_NAME 32a -set _NET_WM_NAME LG3D
or
.B wmname LG3D
(to pretend that a non-reparenting window manager is running that the
XToolkit/XAWT backend can recognize) or when using OpenJDK setting the environment variable
.BR _JAVA_AWT_WM_NONREPARENTING=1 .
.SH BUGS
Send all bug reports with a patch to hackers@suckless.org.
